The [#4471 page](/kb/incidents/4471) says one issue with one fix queued. Its own table says
otherwise. This page splits the ticket by cause and points each cause to its own ticket.

<Callout kind="warn">
#4471 is two bugs. A parser fix closes only one of them.
</Callout>

## What #4471 holds

<CauseTable ticket={4471} />

Every count above comes from the export at render time, so it stays true as the desk merges more
reports. The cause comes from the reporter's tenant: its locale, and the booking form it runs
(`causes.ts`). The intake tag is a guess from the reporter's browser. The "tagged as" column shows
how far it is from the cause, so do not count by tag.

## One ticket per cause

| cause | ticket | what breaks |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `parser` | [#1](https://github.com/mjmazgaj/mega-s01e03-what-it-shows/issues/1) | The shared parser reads every date month first, and every tenant on file writes the day first. |
| `hint` | [#2](https://github.com/mjmazgaj/mega-s01e03-what-it-shows/issues/2) | The booking-v3 form shows `MM/DD/YYYY` under its date field, then reads the date day first. Only reports sent through that form. |

The report counts are in the table above and in each ticket.

## The one-day moves

The note under the table lists reports the desk read as a one-day move (`desk-notes.json`). The kb
files them by tenant, under `parser`, and #1 counts them there. Their text describes a day lost,
not a day and month swapped. Check them on their own before #1 is closed.

## Why it was merged as one

[Decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject) merges reports whose subject lines match.
Both bugs produce the same subjects ("wrong day", "date is wrong"), so the subject rule put two
causes in one ticket. Engineering then saw one bug and queued one fix.

0001 is accepted, so it stays as it is. Its review date is past the stale limit. A new decision
record should replace it with a rule that merges on cause (`sameCause` in `causes.ts`), and the
[triage page](/kb/triage/dedupe) should point to that new record.

